    - dogfooding  # 2322
    isbefore: []  # 2322
    duplicate_of: []  # 2322
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 2322
    pr_ref:  # 2322
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 9).**\n\n**Symptom.** Hypergumbo declares fingerprint schemes at the top level of every behavior-map:\n\n```json\n\"repo_fingerprint_scheme\": \"hypergumbo-repofp-v1\",\n\"shape_id_scheme\":         \"hypergumbo-shapeid-v1\",\n\"stable_id_scheme\":        \"hypergumbo-stableid-v2\",\n```\n\nBut the fingerprint fields they reference are almost entirely null on hypergumbo's self-analysis:\n\n| Producer | Count | non-null fingerprint |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `analysis_runs[*].repo_fingerprint` | 133 | **0** (0.0%) |\n| `nodes[*].fingerprint` | 32,253 | **90** (0.3%) |\n\nThe 90 nodes with non-null fingerprints break down as:\n- 81 `dependency` nodes from `toml-v1` (parsed pyproject.toml deps)\n- 7 `project` nodes from `toml-v1` (workspace packages)\n- 1 `package` node from `json-v1`\n- 1 `file` node from `json-v1`\n\nIn other words: **every source-code Symbol (functions, classes, methods, modules) has `fingerprint = None`**, and **every analysis run has `repo_fingerprint = None`**, despite the schemes being declared at the schema level.\n\n**Why this is a problem.**\n\nThe schemes' existence implies fingerprints are first-class identity. Documentation and adjacent code treat them as a real signal — but they're a no-op for 99.7% of the data. An agent or tool that:\n- Joins on `(stable_id, fingerprint)` to disambiguate symbol collisions gets no help from fingerprint\n- Filters edges where `node.fingerprint == X` matches 90 nodes out of 32K\n- Detects \"did the repo content change since last analysis?\" by `repo_fingerprint` always sees `None` and falls back to other signals\n\nConsumers can't tell from the JSON alone that fingerprints are essentially vestigial for source-code nodes.\n\n**Two ways this could be wrong:**\n\n1. **Producers should emit fingerprints, but don't.** The schemes exist because someone planned for fingerprints to be ubiquitous, but only the manifest-parsing analyzers actually compute them. Source-code analyzers (py.py, js, go, rust, …) should compute a content-hash or similar fingerprint per Symbol.\n\n2. **The schema declares a feature that doesn't exist.** The `repo_fingerprint_scheme` field is documentation rot — there's no plan to actually populate it. In that case the schema fields should be removed, or the docs should clarify \"currently only populated for dependency/project nodes parsed from manifests; null for all source-code symbols.\"\n\nWithout knowing project intent (closer to option 1 from the field naming, but option 2 may be the pragmatic choice), I'm filing this as a work item rather than an invariant violation.\n\n**Severity.** Low-medium. Doesn't break consumers, but misleads them about what identity signals are available. Same pattern as WI-luvig (`canonical_name` docstring oversells the field): a declared field is mostly null in practice, and there's no warning to the consumer.\n\n**Fix proposal.**\n\n**Option A — populate fingerprints for source-code nodes.** Compute a deterministic content-hash per Symbol (e.g., sha256 of normalized source text within the span) and stamp `fingerprint`. Requires a structural change in every analyzer. High effort.\n\n**Option B — document the current scope.** Update spec/schema docs:\n- \"`repo_fingerprint_scheme` is reserved for future use. Currently `analysis_runs[*].repo_fingerprint` is always null.\"\n- \"`nodes[*].fingerprint` is populated only for `dependency`, `project`, `package`, and `file` nodes parsed from manifest files (pyproject.toml, package.json). Source-code symbols (function/class/method/module) have `fingerprint = None`.\"\n\nRecommended: B as an interim while A is scoped. A should probably be a separate, larger work item if/when fingerprints are wanted as first-class symbol identity.\n\n**Test gap.** Property test: `assert (n.kind in {\"dependency\", \"project\", \"package\", \"file\"}) or (n.fingerprint is None)`. This would catch any future divergence — e.g., if an analyzer starts emitting non-null fingerprints on function nodes without updating the docs.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/ir.py` (Symbol dataclass — fingerprint field definition)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/limits.py:179` (analyzer_version producer)\n- `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md` (schema-level scheme documentation — needs to clarify scope)\n- `docs/schema.json` (declares the scheme fields)\n- `WI-luvig-...` (filed round 7 — `canonical_name` docstring oversells, same pattern)\n- `INV-sopon-...` (filed round 2 — linker-Symbol `origin` not stamped, sibling identity-field issue)\n- `INV-fusus-...` (filed round 4 — `stable_id` 91% collision; this finding gives context why joining on `(stable_id, fingerprint)` to disambiguate wouldn't help)"  # 2322
    fields: {}  # 2322
